/target
# A member's tests fall back to `<member>/target` when the temp folder is
# unusable (src/scratch.rs), so every package can grow one of its own —
# build output and scratch both, never source.
**/target/
# Only the wasm build output is disposable (web/build.sh regenerates
# it); the page and the web scripts beside it are source — keep them
# tracked so any machine can `rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown
# && cargo install wasm-bindgen-cli --version 0.2.129 && ./web/build.sh`.
/web/pkg/
/antifrost-web.zip
# Cargo.lock is tracked: the wasm-bindgen CLI must match the crate the
# lock resolves (see web/build.sh), examples want reproducible builds,
# and a resolver's opinion is cheap to version and expensive to re-guess.
**/.DS_Store

# The game writes its own files where it launches from — settings the
# moment one changes, progress whenever a hill moves. Per-machine
# artifacts, never source.
antifrost.settings.ron
antifrost.progress.ron

# self-signed certificates for `serve.py --tls` — minted locally, never committed
web/.tls/
web/.watch-stamp

# The probe pages under web/ are source; their wasm is build output.
/web/min/pkg/
/web/place/pkg/

# Scratch worktrees and a hardlinked cargo home for building old commits.
/.workspace/

# tools/ holds python now, and driving a browser means importing the tools
# from scratch scripts as well as running them: bytecode lands next to a
# tracked file on the first import and is never wanted.
__pycache__/

# A cargo home is a cache, never source. The reason to name it explicitly rather
# than trust "nobody would commit a cache": the plugin project this engine is
# being joined by keeps its cargo home in the tree, and that directory holds a
# full unpacked Steinberg ASIO SDK — headers, the licensing agreement, the logo
# artwork, 48 files and 10 MB of it. Steinberg's licence for that SDK is
# proprietary, incompatible with this repository's `MIT OR Apache-2.0`, and it
# is fetched by a build script into exactly this path (`CPAL_ASIO_DIR`). One
# `git add -A` beside such a tree would publish someone else's SDK from a public
# MIT repository, which is a thing a repository cannot undo by deleting a commit.
.cargohome/
